  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Stay safe on the ground, stop active interior leaks, and document all damage with photos before calling a trusted local roofer and your insurance company.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Stay safe on the ground, stop active interior leaks, and document all damage with photos before calling a trusted local roofer and your insurance company"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Immediate Steps (First 24 Hours)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Prioritize safety: Wait until the storm has completely passed, stay clear of downed power lines, and do not climb onto a wet or damaged roof.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Inspect from the ground: Use binoculars or your phone camera to check for missing shingles, bent gutters, or fallen tree limbs.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Check the attic and ceilings: Look for active drips, wet insulation, sagging ceilings, or daylight shining through the roof deck. Turn off the electrical breaker if water is near light fixtures or outlets.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Prevent further water damage: Place buckets under leaks, move furniture and valuables away from water, and arrange for emergency tarping or temporary dry-in if the roof is exposed.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Document everything: Take wide and close-up photos and videos of roof edges, debris, broken items, and interior water stains before you clean up or move anything.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Next Steps (Assess & File)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Record storm details: Write down the exact date, time, and type of severe weather (hail, high winds, heavy rain).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Call a reputable local roofer: Get a professional inspection to identify hidden structural or shingle damage. Beware of door-to-door storm chasers demanding upfront cash or full contract signatures on the spot.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              4,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Contact your insurance company: File a claim promptly\u2014check your policy details to see whether you are covered for Replacement Cost Value (RCV) or Actual Cash Value (ACV), which factors in roof age and depreciation.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              6
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "What type of damage you see (missing shingles, leaks, fallen branches)"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "How old your roof is"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can help you prepare for your insurance call or roofer estimate."
      }
    ],
